  "step_3": "The decision reason focuses on preferring the lottery *without* a sure outcome, which would be Lottery B in this case. The individual, however, chooses Lottery A, which has a sure outcome. More importantly, their justification centers on the *magnitude* of potential losses and risk proportionality, not on the presence or absence of a sure outcome as a decision rule. While they do *observe* that A has a sure loss, this is noted descriptively, not used as a reason to avoid it. In fact, they favor the sure outcome despite acknowledging it, suggesting the decision reason (which would favor B for lacking a sure outcome) was not guiding their choice. There is no indication that the absence of a sure outcome in B was seen as an advantage. Thus, neither the relevant information (structural certainty) nor the rule (prefer no sure outcome) aligns with the individual’s deliberation. The decision reason was not used.",
  "final_assessment_score": 0
}

